body:
`kalide check [--all] [--json]` validates a deck without serving it: it loads and validates the resolved template library layers (templates-dir-validation) and the deck's theme, and applies the whole-deck validation rules to the deck's slides (whole-deck-validation) — config, filenames, frontmatter, sections, fields, bodies, links and images. It opens no server, binds no port and reaches no network (single-binary). It prints a clear summary of the outcome. Exit codes are 0 when the deck is valid, 1 when a validation error is found, and 2 for a usage error; the codes are the same with and without `--json`. By default check is fail-fast: it reports the first error in the fixed whole-deck-validation order. With `--all` it collects every error instead, reporting them all in that same fixed order. Every error has the error-reporting format (error-reporting) — file, line when known, path, what is wrong and how to fix it, with a closest-match suggestion where one applies (closest-match-suggestions) — and the report uses the same text terminal and browser show. With `--json` the report is machine-readable: a valid boolean, a summary, and each error as an object carrying its file, line, path, message (what is wrong and how to fix it) and suggestion (the closest-match hint where one applies). `--json` follows the same collection mode: without `--all` the report holds at most one error (the first), and with `--all` every error. In text mode the summary goes to stdout and the errors go to stderr; with `--json` the report goes to stdout. A usage error is a plain-text message on stderr even with `--json`, and produces no report. The command is the non-serving validation entry point for a pre-commit hook or CI, usable to validate a library's example files and a deck.

AC:
- on a valid deck `kalide check` prints a summary and exits 0;
- on a broken deck it prints the first error in the error-reporting format (error-reporting) and exits 1;
- `kalide check --all` on a deck carrying several errors reports every error, in the fixed whole-deck-validation order, and exits 1;
- `kalide check --json` on a valid deck emits a report with `valid: true` and exits 0; on a deck carrying several errors it emits at most one error (the first) and exits 1;
- `kalide check --json --all` emits a report with every error, in the fixed whole-deck-validation order, and exits 1;
- exit codes are the same with and without `--json` (0 valid, 1 validation error, 2 usage error);
- with `--json` the report goes to stdout; in text mode the summary goes to stdout and the errors go to stderr;
- a usage error with `--json` writes a plain-text message to stderr, produces no report, and exits 2;
- a malformed flag or an unexpected argument is a usage error, exit 2;
- `kalide check` binds no port and opens no browser.
